---

<Layout
  title={`First Flight (Poetry) | CBSE Class 10 English Language & Literature | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Master all 10 poems in CBSE Class 10 English First Flight: Dust of Snow, Fire and Ice, A Tiger in the Zoo, How to Tell Wild Animals, The Ball Poem, Amanda!, The Trees, Fog, Custard the Dragon, and For Anne Gregory with rhyme schemes, poetic devices, visual diagrams, and 20 board MCQs."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / First Flight Poetry
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · English Language &amp; Literature (Code 184) · First Flight</span>
      <h1>First Flight: Poetry (All 10 Poems &amp; Poetic Devices)</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Exhaustive analysis of all 10 poems from the core reader: stanza-by-stanza meanings, symbolic depth, rhyme schemes, figures of speech (metaphor, enjambment, oxymoron, alliteration), and a 20-question CBSE Board Exam quiz.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>First Flight Poetry: Complete Poem Breakdown</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>❄️ 1. Dust of Snow (Robert Frost)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: abab cdcd · Inversion of Inauspicious Symbols</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Occurrence:</strong> The poet is in a depressed, regretful mood ("rued"). A crow shakes down fine particles of snow ("dust of snow") from a poisonous hemlock tree onto the poet as he walks beneath.</li>
            <li><strong>Symbolic Subversion:</strong> Both the crow (symbol of ill-omen and harsh cawing) and the hemlock tree (poisonous plant with white flowers) are conventionally associated with death and sadness. Frost subverts them into agents of healing and natural joy.</li>
            <li><strong>Transformation:</strong> The simple, unexpected natural touch saves "some part of a day" from being wasted in despondency.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "Has <u>g</u>iven my heart", "And <u>s</u>aved <u>s</u>ome part"
              <br/>• <em>Synecdoche:</em> "Heart" represents the entire self and psychological state
              <br/>• <em>Enjambment:</em> The entire poem is composed of two unbroken sentences flowing across stanzas
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔥 2. Fire and Ice (Robert Frost)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: aba abc bcb · Apocalyptic Human Passions</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Dual Causes of Doom:</strong> Contemplates the scientific and moral debate on how the world will end—consumed by fiery nuclear passions or freezing into an icy cosmic standstill.</li>
            <li><strong>Symbolism:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Fire:</em> Represents burning desires, greed, avarice, unbridled lust, and explosive rage.
              <br/>• <em>Ice:</em> Represents cold hatred, rigidity, emotional indifference, prejudice, and insensitivity.
            </li>
            <li><strong>The Poet's Verdict:</strong> Having tasted desire, Frost holds with those who favor fire; but if the world had to perish twice, cold hatred (ice) is equally potent and "would suffice."</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Anaphora:</em> "Some say the world will end in fire / Some say in ice"
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "<u>f</u>avor <u>f</u>ire", "<u>s</u>ome <u>s</u>ay"
              <br/>• <em>Personification:</em> Fire and ice endowed with the capacity to destroy civilization
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🐅 3. A Tiger in the Zoo (Leslie Norris)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: abcb · The Tragedy of Caged Wildlife</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Caged Reality:</strong> Stalking in his vivid stripes within a narrow concrete cell, taking only a few steps on pads of velvet quiet, suppressing his fierce majesty in "quiet rage."</li>
            <li><strong>The Natural Habitat (Antithesis):</strong> In the wild, he should be lurking in shadow, sliding through long grass near the water hole where plump deer pass, or snarling around village houses at the jungle's edge, baring his white fangs to terrorize naturally.</li>
            <li><strong>The Night Loneliness:</strong> Confined behind bars, his strength locked up, he ignores passing visitors and stares with his brilliant eyes at the brilliant, unreachable stars at night.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Oxymoron:</em> "quiet rage" (quietness contradicts rage)
              <br/>• <em>Metaphor:</em> "pads of velvet quiet" (soft paws compared to smooth velvet)
              <br/>• <em>Personification:</em> Referring to the tiger as "He" rather than "It"
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "<u>b</u>ehind <u>b</u>ars", "<u>p</u>lump deer <u>p</u>ass"
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🦁 4. How to Tell Wild Animals (Carolyn Wells)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: ababcc · Humorous Taxonomy of Danger</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Satirical Premise:</strong> Offers humorous, life-threatening guidelines on how to identify wild beasts in the eastern jungles—where the identification only occurs as the beast attacks you!</li>
            <li><strong>The Beasts &amp; Clues:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Asian Lion:</em> Large, tawny beast whose roar kills you with fright.
              <br/>• <em>Bengal Tiger:</em> Noble beast with black stripes on yellow ground that eats you as soon as you meet him.
              <br/>• <em>Leopard:</em> Peppered with spots that leaps and leaps again ("lep and lep again"), impervious to cries of pain.
              <br/>• <em>Bear:</em> Hugs you very, very hard in a lethal embrace.
              <br/>• <em>Crocodile vs. Hyena:</em> Hyenas come with merry smiles; crocodiles weep real tears while swallowing prey.
              <br/>• <em>Chameleon:</em> Small lizard without ears or wings; if there is nothing on the tree, "tis the chameleon you see" (camouflage).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Poetic Licence:</em> Deliberate misspelling ("lep and lep") to maintain meter
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "<u>n</u>ovice might <u>n</u>onplus"
              <br/>• <em>Irony &amp; Humour:</em> Treating mortal danger as lighthearted field etiquette
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚽ 5. The Ball Poem (John Berryman)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Blank Verse (Unrhymed) · The Epistemology of Loss</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Incident:</strong> A young boy happily bouncing his ball watches it bounce merrily down the street and splash into the dark waters of the harbor.</li>
            <li><strong>The Grief:</strong> The boy stands trembling and rigid, staring down where his ball went. It is not just a rubber ball that is lost; all his young childhood memories are swallowed by the harbor.</li>
            <li><strong>Epistemology of Loss:</strong> "Money is external." A dime can buy another ball, but a replacement ball cannot buy back the lost psychological association. The boy is sensing his "first responsibility" in a world of possessions.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Anaphora:</em> "<u>What, what</u> is he to do?", "<u>Merrily</u> bouncing... <u>Merrily</u> over"
              <br/>• <em>Metaphor:</em> "All his young days into the harbour" (ball's loss symbolizes loss of innocent youth)
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "<u>b</u>uys a <u>b</u>all <u>b</u>ack"
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧜‍♀️ 6. Amanda! (Robin Klein)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: aaba ccc aada eee · Autonomy vs. Constant Nagging</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Reality (Parental Commands):</strong> Don't bite your nails, don't hunch your shoulders, finish your homework, clean your room, don't eat that chocolate, remember your acne, stop sulking!</li>
            <li><strong>Amanda's Dreamscapes (Parentheses):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>The Mermaid:</em> Drifting blissfully alone in a languid emerald sea where the sole inhabitant is her.
              <br/>• <em>The Orphan:</em> Roaming barefoot in the street, making patterns in soft dust with bare feet; "Silence is golden, the freedom is sweet."
              <br/>• <em>Rapunzel:</em> Living tranquil in a high solitary tower, but swearing she will "never let down her bright hair" so that no intruder can disrupt her peace.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Theme:</strong> The stifling effect of over-parenting that forces imaginative children into total escapist isolation.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Allusion:</em> Mermaid, Rapunzel
              <br/>• <em>Metaphor:</em> "Silence is golden, the freedom is sweet"
              <br/>• <em>Anaphora:</em> "<u>Don't</u> bite... <u>Don't</u> hunch..."
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌳 7. The Trees (Adrienne Rich)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Free Verse · Emancipation of Women &amp; Nature's Rebellion</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Confinement:</strong> Trees that have been confined inside the decorative veranda of human houses for years are now stirring to move out into the barren forest where birds could not sit and insects could not hide.</li>
            <li><strong>The Struggle:</strong>
              <br/>• Roots work all night to disengage themselves from the veranda floor cracks.
              <br/>• Leaves strain toward the glass; small twigs stiffen with exertion; boughs move like newly discharged hospital patients stumbling half-dazed to clinic doors.
            </li>
            <li><strong>The Metaphor:</strong> The poem operates on two levels: ecological restoration of exploited forests, and a powerful feminist allegory of women breaking free from domestic confinement into the open world of leadership and agency.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Simile:</em> "like newly discharged patients", "moon is broken like a mirror"
              <br/>• <em>Personification:</em> "roots work", "leaves strain", "forest stumbles"
              <br/>• <em>Enjambment:</em> Free-flowing lines mimicking unrestrained natural movement
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌫️ 8. Fog (Carl Sandburg)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Free Verse · Subtle Impressionist Metaphor</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Imagery:</strong> In just six concise lines, Sandburg captures the quiet arrival of atmospheric fog over Chicago harbor and the city.</li>
            <li><strong>The Extended Metaphor (Cat):</strong>
              <br/>• The fog comes "on little cat feet" — silent, stealthy, and unannounced.
              <br/>• It sits "looking over harbour and city on silent haunches" — just like a cat perches observing its territory without making a sound.
              <br/>• And then "moves on" — dispersing cleanly into the air without leaving a trace.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Core Meaning:</strong> Nature's transformative phenomena arrive and depart silently without human intervention.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Extended Metaphor:</em> Fog compared entirely to an observant feline
              <br/>• <em>Enjambment:</em> One single flowing thought divided across two brief stanzas
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🐉 9. The Tale of Custard the Dragon (Ogden Nash)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: aabb · Mock-Heroic Ballad on True Courage</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Belinda's Household:</strong>
              <br/>• Belinda (brave as a barrel full of bears)
              <br/>• Ink (little black kitten, chased lions down the stairs)
              <br/>• Blink (little gray mouse)
              <br/>• Mustard (little yellow dog, brave as a tiger in a rage)
              <br/>• Custard (the coward dragon who only cried for a nice safe cage)
            </li>
            <li><strong>The Crisis:</strong> A nasty pirate with a wooden leg and pistols in both hands breaks through the window. Belinda pales and cries "Help! Help!"; Mustard flees yelping; Ink trickles into the cellar; Blink scuttles into a mousehole.</li>
            <li><strong>The Turnaround:</strong> The cowardly Custard jumps up snorting like an engine, clashes his tail like irons in a dungeon, and charges the pirate like a robin at a worm, gobbling him down whole!</li>
            <li><strong>The Satirical Ending:</strong> Once safe, everyone embraces Custard, but immediately makes excuses ("I would have been twice as brave if I hadn't been flustered!"). Custard humbly agrees that everyone is braver than him, returning to ask for his nice safe cage.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Simile:</em> "snorting like an engine", "clashed his tail like irons"
              <br/>• <em>Onomatopoeia:</em> "meowch!", "weeck!", "clatter", "clank"
              <br/>• <em>Oxymoron:</em> "pet dragon"
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>💛 10. For Anne Gregory (William Butler Yeats)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Rhyme Scheme: abcbdb · Spiritual Love vs. Superficial Beauty</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Dilemma:</strong> A young man is thrown into despair not by Anne Gregory's inner character, but by her gorgeous "yellow hair" falling like honey-coloured ramparts by her ear.</li>
            <li><strong>The Physical Appearance:</strong> Anne insists that she can easily dye her hair brown, black, or carrot so that young men might love her for herself alone and not her hair.</li>
            <li><strong>The Divine Epiphany:</strong> The speaker quotes an old religious text found by an ascetic: only God possesses the capacity to love a human for themselves alone and not for their superficial yellow hair. Human love remains inherently bound by physical attractions.</li>
            <li><strong>Poetic Devices:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Metaphor:</em> "honey-coloured ramparts" (thick hair framed like castle defensive walls)
              <br/>• <em>Alliteration:</em> "<u>y</u>ellow <u>y</u>our", "<u>l</u>ove you for your<u>s</u>elf a<u>l</u>one"
              <br/>• <em>Synecdoche:</em> "Yellow hair" represents outward physical beauty
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Symbolic Dichotomies in Class 10 Poetry</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Comparative Analysis Matrix</span>
          <h3>Fire and Ice: The Anatomy of Human Self-Destruction</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Fire Column -->
              <rect x="30" y="30" width="250" height="260" rx="10" fill="#fff1f2" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="155" y="65" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#be123c">🔥 FIRE (Desire)</text>
              <line x1="50" y1="80" x2="260" y2="80" stroke="#fda4af" stroke-width="1.5" />
              <text x="55" y="110" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#9f1239">• Burning Ambition &amp; Greed</text>
              <text x="55" y="140" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#9f1239">• Uncontrolled Lust &amp; Passion</text>
              <text x="55" y="170" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#9f1239">• Aggressive Impulsive Fury</text>
              <text x="55" y="200" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#9f1239">• Rapacious Material Conquest</text>
              <rect x="50" y="230" width="210" height="40" rx="6" fill="#ffe4e6" />
              <text x="155" y="255" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#881337">End Result: Rapid Incineration</text>

              <!-- Ice Column -->
              <rect x="320" y="30" width="250" height="260" rx="10" fill="#f0f9ff" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="445" y="65" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">❄️ ICE (Hatred)</text>
              <line x1="340" y1="80" x2="550" y2="80" stroke="#bae6fd" stroke-width="1.5" />
              <text x="345" y="110" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0c4a6e">• Cold Emotional Indifference</text>
              <text x="345" y="140" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0c4a6e">• Deep-seated Malice &amp; Apathy</text>
              <text x="345" y="170" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0c4a6e">• Rigid Prejudices &amp; Intolerance</text>
              <text x="345" y="200" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0c4a6e">• Insensitivity to Others' Pain</text>
              <rect x="340" y="230" width="210" height="40" rx="6" fill="#e0f2fe" />
              <text x="445" y="255" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#075985">End Result: Silent Annihilation</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 1: Robert Frost balances emotional heat against moral coldness as twin forces of apocalypse.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Spatial &amp; Emotional Contrast</span>
          <h3>A Tiger in the Zoo: Habitat vs. Concrete Prison</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Left: The Zoo Cell -->
              <rect x="30" y="30" width="250" height="260" rx="8" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="155" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#334155">Locked in Concrete Cell</text>
              <!-- Cell bars -->
              <line x1="70" y1="80" x2="70" y2="200" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              <line x1="110" y1="80" x2="110" y2="200" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              <line x1="150" y1="80" x2="150" y2="200" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              <line x1="190" y1="80" x2="190" y2="200" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              <line x1="230" y1="80" x2="230" y2="200" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="3" />
              <text x="155" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#475569">• Stalks few steps in quiet rage</text>
              <text x="155" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#475569">• Ignores visitors; stares at stars</text>
              <text x="155" y="270" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#64748b">Strength locked behind bars</text>

              <!-- Center VS -->
              <circle cx="300" cy="160" r="22" fill="#e2e8f0" stroke="#94a3b8" />
              <text x="300" y="166" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#1e293b">VS</text>

              <!-- Right: The Wild Habitat -->
              <rect x="320" y="30" width="250" height="260" rx="8" fill="#f0fdf4" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="445" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#15803d">Natural Forest Freedom</text>
              <path d="M 350 180 Q 380 90 410 180 T 470 180 T 530 180" fill="none" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="3" />
              <text x="445" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#166534">Lurking in deep shadow</text>
              <text x="445" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#166534">Sliding through long grass</text>
              <text x="445" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#166534">• Near water hole where deer pass</text>
              <text x="445" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#166534">• Snarling at jungle edges</text>
              <text x="445" y="270" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#15803d">Terrorizing with white fangs &amp; claws</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 2: Leslie Norris's spatial juxtaposition highlighting the injustice of wildlife confinement.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quiz Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Practice: First Flight Poetry</h2>
          <p class="muted">20 High-yield MCQs covering extract identification, rhyme schemes, poetic devices, and symbolic allusions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizApp"></div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <span class="muted" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
        <button class="button secondary" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

